  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat builds this week. High confidence in above\nnormal temperatures.\n\nA strong upper ridge will begin to strengthen over the southern\nUnited States this week. This ridge will continue to build east\nthrough the week, leading to multiple days of upper 80s to \nupper 90s over the region.\n\nThe heat should peak Wednesday, but remains quite high Thursday\nas well, especially over the Piedmont. Temperatures in the upper \n80s and upper 90s combined with dew points in the mid to upper \n60s will result in heat indices in the lower 100s for parts of \nthe Piedmont as well as the Roanoke urban area during this \nperiod. At this time, heat index values still remain below Heat\nAdvisory criteria... However those sensitive to the heat should\nlimit their time outdoors this week.\n\nRain chances remain low this week with perhaps a few tiny cells\ntrying to develop in the afternoons and early evenings. Best \nchance of storms will be along the southern Blue Ridge, but \nstrong subsidence under the ridge will prevent anything more \nthan shallow convection through Thursday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cooler temperatures and an increasing chance of\nrain/storms by the late this week into the weekend.\n\nTroughing over the northeast by Friday will amplify resulting \nin a greater chance for rain/storms for the weekend as the \nstrong ridging moves west over the Rockies. Increased forcing\nfrom an approaching front should introduce better coverage of\nafternoon and evening storms in a moderately unstable atmosphere\nas boundary layer moisture increases ahead of the front.\nHowever, best shear/instability overlap still remains closer to\nthe Mason- Dixon line Friday, but a few stronger storms could \nbe possible across portions of the forecast area. The front will\ncontinue to push south and the best chance for rain and storms \nappears to occur on Saturday with the actual passage of the cold\nfront. \n\nCooler and less humid airmass begins to work into the region by\nlate in the weekend and early next week, but in-situ wedging may\nkeep some showers and clouds around behind the frontal passage\nthrough at least early Monday.",
